Feel the bounce: rumble the pad where the bounce sound is mixed.

A ball game on a console with no haptic feedback is missing an obvious beat, and the
event to hang it on already exists - `sol_step` returns the bounce strength, upstream
turns it into `k` and mixes the sound at that volume, and this passes the same `k` to the
pad. Deriving a separate intensity here would let a hard hit sound loud and feel soft;
sharing `k` makes the speaker and the motors agree by construction.

The patch is one call and one include, because everything hard about rumble is on the
other side of it. `oops_input_set_rumble` sets a *state* - the motors run until they are
told otherwise - so a bump needs something to turn it off afterwards, and that is a decay
thread in `common/haptics.c`, shared by every port rather than written per title. The
thread is started and stopped by the shim around `main`, so neither of those is a patch
either.

`common/` is on the include path for this build, which is why the header resolves.

diff --git a/ball/game_server.c b/ball/game_server.c
index 4eb1102..6d7dd19 100644
--- a/ball/game_server.c
+++ b/ball/game_server.c
@@ -29,6 +29,10 @@
 #include "game_server.h"
 #include "game_proxy.h"
 
+/* Pad rumble on a bounce - the port's, shared with every other title here rather than written
+   per game. `common/` is on the include path for this build. */
+#include "haptics.h"
+
 #include "cmd.h"
 
 /*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
@@ -817,6 +821,14 @@ static int game_step(const float g[3], float dt, int bt)
             {
                 float k = (b - 0.5f) * 2.0f;
 
+                /* **The same event, felt as well as heard.** `k` is the bounce strength the
+                 * sound is already mixed at, so the pad and the speaker agree by construction -
+                 * deriving a separate intensity here would let a hard hit look loud and feel
+                 * soft. Turning the motors off again is not this file's problem: see
+                 * `common/haptics.h`, whose decay thread owns that and is started by the
+                 * shim. */
+                oops_haptics_bump(k);
+
                 if (got_orig)
                 {
                     if      (vary.uv->r > grow_orig) audio_play(AUD_BUMPL, k);
